<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
"-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
"qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
Reusing a session could allow an attacker to gain unauthorized access to another account.
Always ensure that, when a user logs in or out, the current session is abandoned so that a new
session may be started.
</p>
<p>
This query also recognizes login handlers that pass the session object to a helper function,
which then marks the session as authenticated without regenerating it.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Always use <code>req.session.regenerate(...);</code> to start a new session when a user logs in or out.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
The following example passes the existing session to a helper that stores the authenticated user
in it. An attacker who planted or learned the session ID before the user logged in can use it to
access the user's account afterwards.
</p>
<sample src="examples/SessionFixationBad.js" />

<p>
The following example solves the problem by calling <code>req.session.regenerate()</code> before
storing the authenticated user, so that the previous session is not reused.
</p>
<sample src="examples/SessionFixationGood.js" />
</example>

<references>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Session_fixation">Session fixation</a>.</li>
<li>Stack Overflow: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22209354/creating-a-new-session-after-authentication-with-passport/30468384#30468384">Creating a new session after authentication with Passport</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/384.html">CWE-384</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
